Project-specific review notes:

This project has two codelines, both pure-TypeScript ESM modules with no external dependencies beyond vitest and typescript. Both use `"strict": true` and `"noEmit": true` in tsconfig. Source lives in `src/`, tests in `test/`. Tests use vitest's `describe`/`it`/`expect` from `'vitest'`.

- mock-a (`src/fares.ts`, `test/fares.test.ts`): Fare logic with boundary comparisons on `rider.age`. The known bug is an off-by-one in the senior concession threshold — `>` vs `>=` at age 65. When reviewing, verify the boundary condition includes age 65 exactly and that a test case for age exactly 65 exists or is added.
- mock-b (`src/schedule.ts`, `test/schedule.test.ts`): Departure-board rendering via a loop over `Stop[]`. The known bug is the loop truncating the final stop (`i < stops.length - 1`). When reviewing, verify the loop iterates all stops including the last one and that a test asserts the final stop name appears in the output.

Commands this project runs:
- Tests: `npx vitest run` (package.json `test` script)
- Type-check: `npx tsc --noEmit` (package.json `build` script)
- No lint is configured.

Traps for this stack:
- Both bugs are boundary/off-by-one errors. Confirm the fix changes the boundary condition itself, not just a test expectation. A test that passes because the assertion was weakened to match the bug is not a fix.
- TypeScript strict mode is on. Any new code must satisfy `tsc --noEmit` with zero errors — no implicit `any`, no unchecked index access assumptions.
- Both modules are pure functions with no I/O. Error handling means validating inputs at the function boundary (e.g. negative ages, empty arrays) only if the existing code already does so; do not demand patterns the codebase does not use.

